steps.body:
A slice of structs is Go's list of records. With a named type, the element literals can drop the type name:

```go
pts := []Point{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}
```

For a one-off record you never reuse, write an anonymous struct type right in the slice type:

```go
players := []struct {
	Name  string
	Score int
}{
	{"Cy", 88},
	{"Ana", 95},
}
```

The classic gotcha: the range value is a **copy** of the element. `for _, p := range players { p.Score += 5 }` changes only `p`, a temporary struct, and the slice stays as it was. To change elements, index the slice: `for i := range players { players[i].Score += 5 }`.

`sort.Slice` sorts by any field. Compare the main key first and fall back to a second key on ties, so the order is fully decided:

```go
sort.Slice(players, func(i, j int) bool {
	if players[i].Score != players[j].Score {
		return players[i].Score > players[j].Score
	}
	return players[i].Name < players[j].Name
})
```

example.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

func main() {
	players := []struct {
		Name  string
		Score int
	}{
		{"Cy", 88},
		{"Ana", 95},
		{"Ben", 88},
	}
	for _, p := range players {
		p.Score += 5 // changes only the copy
	}
	fmt.Println(players)

	for i := range players {
		players[i].Score += 5
	}
	sort.Slice(players, func(i, j int) bool {
		if players[i].Score != players[j].Score {
			return players[i].Score > players[j].Score
		}
		return players[i].Name < players[j].Name
	})
	for i, p := range players {
		fmt.Printf("%d. %s %d\n", i+1, p.Name, p.Score)
	}
}

